WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.780
Welcome back to another video with
ItsDrDan, and we're gonna be looking

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:09.570
at dehydration reactions, which
are a type of elimination reaction

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.080
of alkanes to make an alkene.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.390
Now, what is dehydration?

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.490
Well, think of hydration, right?

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.800
Hydration is adding water to your body.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.099
In this case, dehydrate is removing water.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.070
So how are we going to make water?

00:00:26.099 --> 00:00:30.119
Well, if you saw the addition
reaction video,  that was also posted

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:36.690
recently, water is all, has an OH
and H component that are on a chain.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:40.830
So what we're going to be looking for
is looking for an OH attached to a

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:46.170
carbon, and then we're gonna be moving
a hydrogen from an adjacent carbon.

00:00:46.769 --> 00:00:54.315
Now, when those are both removed, We're
gonna make H2O as a product and as a

00:00:54.315 --> 00:01:00.405
result, it's going to make a double
bond to,  adjust for the fact that we

00:01:00.435 --> 00:01:03.225
don't have all of our bonds remaining.

00:01:03.825 --> 00:01:09.255
So we will have that c H two
on one carbon, and then we will

00:01:09.255 --> 00:01:11.835
add our hydrogens to the other.

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:17.095
So this goes from an alkane to an alkene.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.759
Through this elimination process.

00:01:21.179 --> 00:01:25.560
So you can also see that it goes
from one reactant to two products.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:29.039
So it's the opposite of
what we saw with addition.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.610
Now with this, you'll commonly
also see that it has an acid

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.130
catalyst in the reaction as well.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.410
So it could be an acid, it could be heat.

00:01:37.679 --> 00:01:40.679
It's something that's showing
that we're having a breaking

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.050
down of our different components.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.980
Let's look at some more
in-depth reactions.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:56.220
The one really common problem that
you're given is form the major

00:01:56.225 --> 00:01:58.690
project from the dehydration reaction.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.125
And when you're looking at
this, you're like, oh man,

00:02:01.185 --> 00:02:03.345
what exactly do I have to do?

00:02:04.005 --> 00:02:08.025
So when we have this general
reaction, what we can see is we are

00:02:08.025 --> 00:02:10.125
always given some kind of reactant.

00:02:10.215 --> 00:02:14.325
And what we are going to be doing
is identifying the major components

00:02:14.325 --> 00:02:15.465
that we're gonna be ripping off.

00:02:15.915 --> 00:02:19.215
And one thing you can remember,
well, our goal is to form water, so

00:02:19.215 --> 00:02:20.865
we're gonna be taking away an OH.

00:02:22.049 --> 00:02:25.620
Now the question is, where do
we remove the hydrogen from?

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:30.090
Because you could remove it
from the carbon on its left

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.910
or the carbon on its right.

00:02:33.299 --> 00:02:41.790
So if we were to fill in all those
hydrogens, we have 1, 2, 3 on the

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:43.950
right hand side, and then one.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:47.670
Two on the left, where do we choose?

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.670
It's kind of a little bit of a
dilemma that we have is like, okay,

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:54.899
do I pick one from over here or do
I pick one on the left hand side?

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.630
So let's write down both those products.

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:01.739
And what we are going to do is go
through another rule to be able to

00:03:01.739 --> 00:03:04.440
define what we, what we will have.

00:03:04.829 --> 00:03:06.000
So it's the same idea.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.670
We copy down our carbon structure.

00:03:09.329 --> 00:03:15.149
And we are going to write that backbone
down because the carbons stay the same.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.609
We're only removing the alcohol.

00:03:18.179 --> 00:03:23.579
So the one that I have here on the,
the upper side here, this is if I

00:03:23.584 --> 00:03:26.820
remove the hydrogen that is in orange.

00:03:27.359 --> 00:03:30.570
So if we remove that one,
it's going to make the double

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.760
bond on the end of the chain.

00:03:33.375 --> 00:03:39.765
If we remove the hydrogen from the
one in red, it will make the double

00:03:39.765 --> 00:03:41.205
bond in the middle of the chain.

00:03:41.205 --> 00:03:45.165
So you can see how there is a big
difference in both of these, right?

00:03:45.165 --> 00:03:50.984
We have pentene on the top, and
over here we have 2-pentene.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:54.990
Which will also have cis
and trans isomers to it.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.960
So there's a big
difference between the two.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:03:59.370
Which one will we have?

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:05.760
Let's look at our rule to determine which
is the correct answer, Seth Rule, which

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:12.090
goes through a lot of scientific verbiage
for how to describe which hydrogen you are

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.880
going to pluck off of this alkene chain.

00:04:15.524 --> 00:04:20.265
The easy way to remember it is think
of predator versus prey mentality.

00:04:20.265 --> 00:04:23.414
How exactly are you going
to look at these structures?

00:04:23.895 --> 00:04:29.115
So with this, it's all about removing
the hydrogen that is more by itself.

00:04:29.505 --> 00:04:33.465
You want to go for the weakest
link in your parent or your chain.

00:04:33.854 --> 00:04:35.955
So you want to find the
one that's by itself.

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:39.390
So let's take a look at the
example we were doing before.

00:04:39.599 --> 00:04:44.520
Let's see if we can see which hydrogen is
an easier target to remove from our chain.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.510
So in this example, we had two
separate chains that we formed.

00:04:48.929 --> 00:04:51.929
Now how do we tell which
one is the lower of the two?

00:04:52.409 --> 00:04:55.590
Well, what we're gonna do is we're gonna
look back on our original structure,

00:04:56.039 --> 00:05:00.960
and if you can see on the left hand
side, I have two hydrogens, and on

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.479
the right hand side I have three.

00:05:03.945 --> 00:05:08.804
So the whole idea here is that we want to
pick the structure that involves the least

00:05:08.804 --> 00:05:12.375
amount of work, which one is by itself,
which in this case you can see that

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:15.375
the reds structure would have only two.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:19.965
So that is gonna be easier
to pluck off and to remove.

00:05:20.534 --> 00:05:23.145
So our correct answer
for our major product.

00:05:24.435 --> 00:05:26.985
Is going to be the bottom species.

00:05:27.465 --> 00:05:31.695
Now, keep in mind when you say major,
that does not mean it's the only product.

00:05:31.695 --> 00:05:37.664
It just tells you that it could be
60%, 70%, 80% of that major product, or

00:05:37.664 --> 00:05:40.225
more, sometimes even as high as a 100%.

00:05:40.635 --> 00:05:43.635
But it all depends on the
conditions of your reaction

00:05:43.635 --> 00:05:45.405
and what you were doing in lab.

